            <ArticleTitle>Henoch-Schönlein purpura as a rare cause of an acute abdomen.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>e88-e90</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1308/rcsann.2016.0359</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 52-year-old man presented with a purpuric rash affecting his legs and hypertension. He was diagnosed with Henoch-Schönlein purpura and discharged with non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs. Three weeks later, he presented again with loss of appetite and vomiting before developing abdominal pain with pyrexia of 38.5°C and rigors. On examination, he was hypotensive with a distended abdomen and a national early warning score of 6. Computed tomography revealed enteropathy of Henoch-Schönlein purpura. The patient deteriorated and went for an emergency laparotomy, where 30cm of ischaemic small bowel was resected.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
